Troubleshooting
Issue: python3 -c "import yaml..." fails with ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'yaml' Diagnostic hint: run pip3 show pyyaml to confirm the package installed for the Python interpreter being invoked; if using a virtual environment, activate it before running the validation script.

Issue: git checkout <tag> -- pipelines/ reports "did not match any file(s) known to git" Diagnostic hint: run git tag --list to confirm the tag name is spelled exactly as created, and confirm the pipelines/ path existed at that tag with git show <tag> --stat.

Conclusion
In this lab, you implemented a practical version control strategy for OpenShift Tekton pipelines, starting from an organized Git repository structure through semantic versioning, environment-specific pipeline variants, automated YAML validation, and a working rollback procedure. You practiced tagging releases with git tag, incrementing versions with a reusable shell script, and recovering a prior pipeline definition using a dedicated rollback branch. These skills translate directly to real CI/CD operations, where reliably tracking, validating, and reverting pipeline changes is essential to keeping deployment automation safe and auditable. As a next step, consider extending the validation script to run in a Git pre-commit hook and wiring the rollback branch workflow into a pull-request-based review process before changes reach production pipelines.
